## Why This Is Asked
Assesses hands-on windowed aggregation, per-key state management, late-data handling, and correct offset commits in a beginner KCNA setup.

## Key Concepts
- Tumbling windows, per-key state, late data handling, TTL, idempotent emits, offset management.

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowedAggSink {
  constructor(windowMs, latenessMs) {
    this.windowMs = windowMs;
    this.latenessMs = latenessMs;
    this.state = new Map(); // windowStart -> deviceId -> sum
  }
  onMessage(msg) {
    // msg: { deviceId, value, timestamp, offset }
    const ws = this.windowStartFor(msg.timestamp);
    const bucket = (this.state.get(ws) = this.state.get(ws) || new Map());
    bucket.set(msg.deviceId, (bucket.get(msg.deviceId) || 0) + msg.value);
  }
  onWindowBoundary(now) {
    // emit and clean up windows that ended before now - latenessMx
  }
  commitOffsets() {
    // persist offsets after successful emits
  }
  getTotals() {
    // return current in-memory totals for inspection
  }
  windowStartFor(ts) {
    return Math.floor(ts / this.windowMs) * this.windowMs;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design production-grade QoS for multi-tenant streaming: dynamic scheduling, backpressure, and observability under mixed workloads.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant isolation and latency budgets
- Windowed credits and weighted fair queuing
- Backpressure and spill-to-disk strategies
- Observability: latency percentiles, fairness metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
class TenantPool {
  constructor() {
    this.credits = new Map(); // tenantId -> credits
    this.queues = new Map(); // tenantId -> queue
  }
  tryConsume(tenantId, ms) {
    const c = this.credits.get(tenantId) || 0;
    if (c + ms <= budgetFor(tenantId)) {
      this.credits.set(tenantId, c + ms);
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle misbehaving tenants that ignore budgets?
- How would you adapt the scheduler to dynamic topic priorities and outages?

